  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 105 AM CDT Thu Oct 29 2026\n\nFriday through Tuesday:\n\nOblong high pressure spanning from southern Manitoba into lower\nMI will reside over the region Friday, leading to light and \nvariable winds inland and an easterly lake breeze. Highs in the \nmiddle 50s to lower 60s are expected. Meanwhile, a 500 mb \ntrough/upper low over the central Great Plains will lift \nnortheastward, allowing mostly cloudy skies to overspread the \nregion. The associated rain shower chances gradually build into \nthe CWA from southwest (late Friday afternoon) to northeast \n(Saturday). Dynamics with this upper trough are looking rather \nweak a this time. The supergeostrophic flow northeast of the \napproaching trough (which we will experience late Friday into \nearly Friday night) features 500 mb flow of only about 35 knots \n(out of the west), trending slower and featuring variable \ndirections as the upper trough moves overhead. \n\nA complete lack of return flow ahead of this system at the \nsurface, east winds off Lake Michigan, and the origin of this \nupper trough (south-central CONUS, no substantial cool air aloft\nand poor lapse rates) combine to keep instability very limited.\nA few rumbles of thunder are not out of the question yet, but \npotential for any stronger storms/organized severe weather is \nlooking very low at this time. The majority of southern\nWisconsin should stay dry through the daytime Friday, followed \nby increasing shower coverage from SW to NE Friday night, then \nsome widespread 30 to 60 percent rain chances continuing \nthrough the daytime Saturday.\n\nThe 500mb trough breaks down across the Midwest through the \nweekend, with some continued slight rain chances (15 to 30\npercent) but predominantly dry weather into Sunday. The 300 and\n500 mb patterns then begin to amplify into early next week, \nfeaturing troughing over the Rocky Mountains and ridging over \nWisconsin, with both features slowly drifting east. \n\nA more organized rain-maker, likely a healthy surface low \npressure system, it thus expected to cross our region at some \npoint early to middle of next week, with the exact details to be\ndetermined. Continued above normal temperatures can be expected\nwith the ridging ahead of this system early next week. \n\nSheppard",
